  "question": {
    "text": "A convex mirror of radius of curvature <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math> forms an image that is half the size of the object. The object distance is :",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "NRxno",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>45</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "fqcpN",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>45</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "M1Wbs",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "uDAxi",
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math>",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>So, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>+</mo><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p><p>For convex mirror, virtual image is formed for real object. Thus, magnification <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>m</mi></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>The magnification can be written as</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>1</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Equation (1) implies that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>v</mi><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></math></p><p>The relation between the object distance, image distance and the focal length of the mirror is given by</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>f</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo> </mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mo>.</mo><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced></math></p><p>Equation (2) implies that</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>15</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>⇒</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>15</mn><mo> </mo><mi>cm</mi></math></p>",
    "image": null
  },
